RETRACTED 2026-09-09 -- its headline conclusion is wrong; see the banner below
MEMORY_STALL cannot see a bank conflict, so H12's named fix does not work as an instrument

*** RETRACTION, same day, by results/aie/bank_stall_observable_npu.log ***
This log concluded that MEMORY_STALL 'cannot be trusted as a bank-conflict signal on this
machine' and that bank_ab_h12's proposed measurement 'should not be run as written'. BOTH
ARE WRONG. On kernels/memory_placement/, a kernel that asserts the two loads are bundled
into one instruction, MEMORY_STALL counts the conflict EXACTLY: 514/1026/2050/4098 events
at trip counts 512/1024/2048/4096 against a constant 2 when the operands are separated, and
the extra cycles match one for one (slope 12.0 vs 11.0, r^2 = 1.0, zero variance across five
dispatches per point).

THE FAULT WAS THIS PROBE'S KERNEL, NOT THE EVENT. Its loop ran at 15.0 cycles per iteration
for 4 loads, far too loose to be issuing its two loads in the same instruction -- and a
same-bank penalty exists only for PAIRED loads. There was no conflict to detect, so reading
zero was correct behaviour rather than a broken counter. Section 3 below names this as one of
three unseparated candidates; it was the right one, and the headline should not have been
written until they were separated.

WHAT STILL STANDS, and is why this file is kept rather than deleted: section 1's three
structural facts (a core's .bss is ~16 KB, lies entirely inside one 16 KB bank, and is NOT
relocated by stack_size, which moves only ObjectFifo buffers), and section 2's finding that
at eight traced events the counters are not reproducible because ACTIVE overflows the 64 KB
trace buffer. The replacement measurement uses four events for exactly that reason.
*** end retraction ***
2026-09-09. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, native
Windows; driver 32.0.20101.3760. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), power mode Default (1.80 GHz
per results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log). No foreign hardware contexts: `xrt-smi examine -r
aie-partitions` reported "No hardware contexts running on device" before the runs.

Commands (from the ironenv; --help carries the setup):
  python3 kernels/bank_placement/bank_stall_probe.py --target 2048 --repeats 3 --arms 0,1
  python3 kernels/bank_placement/bank_stall_probe.py --target 512 --repeats 3 --arms 0,1 \
      --events INSTR_EVENT_0,INSTR_EVENT_1,MEMORY_STALL,INSTR_LOAD
  ... the same at --target 256, 1024, 2048

WHY: results/aie/bank_ab_h12_npu.log moved the int8 GEMM's operands out of a shared 16 KB bank
exactly as designed and could NOT resolve the effect -- seven alternating series, the sign
changing between them, because wall time on a shared machine cannot see a ~3% core-side change
(96 cycles of a 3,274-cycle call). Its closing paragraph names the fix: "point kernels/pmu_probe/'s
event routing at one core of this design and read ACTIVE against LOCK_STALL in core cycles,
inside the dispatch, where host contention cannot reach."

That plan has an untested premise. A same-bank dual-load conflict should surface as MEMORY_STALL,
and results/aie/pmu_probe_npu.log states plainly that MEMORY_STALL, STREAM_STALL and
CASCADE_STALL "are not shown to work by this run. A kernel that uses them is needed before any
of those three can be trusted." So MEMORY_STALL = 0 on the GEMM's two arms could not distinguish
"no conflict" from "the event does not fire", and a whole sitting would have bought nothing.
This is the positive control that had to come first. There is no BANK_CONFLICT event to fall
back on: the CoreEvent enum this toolchain ships has GROUP_STALL 22, MEMORY_STALL 23,
LOCK_STALL 26, ACTIVE 28, DM_ACCESS_TO_UNAVAILABLE 66 and nothing bank-specific.

METHOD: kernels/bank_placement/bank_stall_kernels.cc runs one loop with two independent 16-lane
int32 loads per iteration -- nothing forces an order, so the scheduler may pair them, and that
pairing is what a same-bank conflict would have to break. One compile-time flag moves stream A's
base and nothing else; the host classifies each run from the ADDRESSES THE KERNEL REPORTS rather
than assuming the intervention landed.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THREE STRUCTURAL FACTS THAT FORCED THE DESIGN, ALL MEASURED HERE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
These are recorded because each one cost an attempt, and each one constrains any future
core-memory experiment on this hardware.

a) A CORE'S .bss IS ABOUT 16 KB, NOT 64 KB. A 32 KB static array is rejected at link time:
   "ld.lld: error: section '.bss' will not fit in region 'data': overflowed by 16640 bytes".
   The tile has 64 KB of local memory; a kernel's own statics get a fraction of it.

b) .bss LIES ENTIRELY INSIDE ONE 16 KB BANK. It was measured spanning 0x75000-0x77C00 -- bank 29
   throughout -- and the input ObjectFifo buffer begins exactly on the next bank boundary,
   0x78000, bank 30. So NO static array can straddle a bank boundary here, and no offset within
   .bss can reach the fifo's bank. A same-bank/different-bank experiment built only from statics
   is impossible by construction, which is why stream B is taken from the fifo buffer.

c) stack_size DOES NOT MOVE A KERNEL'S .bss. bank_ab_h12 shifted the GEMM's operands with it, so
   it was the obvious lever. At stack 0x400 and 0x1400 the streams landed at 0x74100/0x76100
   BOTH times -- identical. The allocator lays ObjectFifo buffers above the stack, which is what
   that log moved; a kernel's own .bss is placed by the linker and the stack does not touch it.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE TRACE UNIT'S COUNTERS ARE NOT REPRODUCIBLE AT EIGHT EVENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First measured with the full eight-event set (INSTR_EVENT_0/1, ACTIVE, MEMORY_STALL, GROUP_STALL,
LOCK_STALL, INSTR_LOAD, INSTR_VECTOR) at target=2048. Three runs of the SAME COMPILED BINARY,
same addresses every time:

  separated arm   cycles 15665, 30724, 15879     INSTR_LOAD 4179, 8195, 4235
  colliding arm   cycles 30730, 30730, 30730     INSTR_LOAD 8195, 8195, 8195

The separated arm's own numbers vary by a factor of two across identical dispatches of identical
code. ACTIVE alone emits 38,000-54,000 frames at this trip count and the trace buffer is 64 KB,
so it overflows and truncates differently per run. ANY conclusion drawn from an eight-event
capture at this loop length would have been an artefact -- including the tempting one that the
colliding arm is 2x slower, which is only the separated arm's counters having been truncated.

Dropping to four events (INSTR_EVENT_0/1, MEMORY_STALL, INSTR_LOAD) makes everything exact and
repeatable. Every figure in section 3 is from that configuration.

GROUP_STALL is worth one line: across all six eight-event runs it read EXACTLY equal to ACTIVE
(38214/38214, 47315/47315, 47329/47329, 53960/53960, 38034/38034, 47255/47255). It is not an
independent stall signal on this part. What it IS was not established: the enum this toolchain
ships carries no docstring for it, and its position (22, immediately before the four specific
stalls at 23-26) merely SUGGESTS a group/OR of them -- suggestive ordering is not documentation,
and no reading of GROUP_STALL is claimed here.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE RESULT: NO PER-ITERATION PENALTY, AND THE EVENT NEVER FIRES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Four events, arms alternating and the order reversed between repeats. The two arms are
INSTRUCTION-MATCHED -- identical INSTR_LOAD -- so cycles are comparable, and every run of a
given arm reproduced its cycle count exactly.

  target   loads/arm    separated (bank 29/30)   colliding (bank 30/30)   gap
     256        1027            3844                     3850              6
     512        2051            7684                     7690              6
    2048        8195           30724                    30730              6

  MEMORY_STALL = 0 in every run of both arms, at every trip count.

THE GAP IS A CONSTANT SIX CYCLES, NOT A RATE. It does not move as the trip count goes 256 ->
512 -> 2048. A one-cycle same-bank dual-load stall per iteration would have cost 256, 512 and
2048 cycles respectively; it costs 6 every time. THE HEADLINE IS THE INVARIANCE, not the cause:
whatever those 6 cycles are, they are paid ONCE, so there is NO per-iteration same-bank
dual-load penalty in this loop. Two causes fit a trip-count-invariant constant and this run
separates neither -- a fixed difference in loop setup (one base pointer is a static address, the
other a function argument), or a one-time bank-arbitration warm-up on first access to the second
bank, which only the separated arm touches. See WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW; do not quote either as
settled.

MEMORY_STALL NEVER FIRES, INCLUDING IN THE ARM BUILT TO COLLIDE. Combined with pmu_probe_npu.log
never having seen it nonzero, the conclusion is the one that matters for H12: MEMORY_STALL
cannot be trusted as a bank-conflict signal on this machine. Reading zero from it on the GEMM's
two arms would not have distinguished "no conflict" from "the event does not fire", so the
measurement bank_ab_h12 proposed would not have resolved H12 either. That plan should not be
run as written.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- THE LOOP IS NOT LOAD-BOUND, and this is the important caveat. It runs at 15.0 cycles per
  iteration (3850/256, 7690/512, 30730/2048 all give 15.0) while issuing 4 loads per iteration.
  A tight, well-scheduled loop would be a small number of cycles per iteration. With that much
  slack the core has room to absorb a one-cycle stall without the wall moving, so this bounds
  the penalty as small IN A LOOP WITH SLACK -- it does not prove there is none in the int8
  GEMM's tight inner loop, which is the case H12 is actually about.
- It does not show WHY MEMORY_STALL reads zero. Three candidates are unseparated: the event may
  not cover bank arbitration; the compiler may not have paired the two loads into one cycle, so
  no conflict was ever created; or the conflict may be resolved without raising the event.
  Reading the disassembly of this loop would separate the second from the other two and has not
  been done.
- One access pattern (two 16-lane int32 loads, 1 KB windows, unit stride), one core tile, one
  power mode. Nothing here covers stores, scalar loads, or the 4-bank striping a real GEMM tile
  sees.
- THE 6-CYCLE CONSTANT HAS TWO CANDIDATE CAUSES AND THIS RUN SEPARATES NEITHER. It is
  attributed above to loop setup -- one base pointer is a static address, the other a function
  argument -- by its INVARIANCE with trip count, not by reading the schedule. The competing
  explanation is a ONE-TIME BANK-ARBITRATION WARM-UP: the separated arm touches two banks where
  the colliding arm touches one, and a fixed cost on first access to the second bank would also
  be trip-count invariant. The data cannot tell them apart, because the separated arm always
  touches two banks. Both readings leave the headline intact -- no PER-ITERATION penalty either
  way -- but neither is ruled out, and the loop-setup reading should not be quoted as settled.
- H12 itself remains NOT confirmed and NOT refuted. This run removes one proposed instrument;
  it does not answer the question that instrument was meant to answer.
